the basic idea is that you know an  ellipse is an oval shape the distance  from the center to the vertices that  distance we call a and it's going to be  either elongated in the vertical  Direction like stretched in the vertical  direction or in the horizontal direction  okay but the distance from the center to  the vertex the vertex vertices are gonna  be on that longer axis that's called a  major axis so in this case this is the  major axis this is the major axis over  here and then the minor axis that's  going to be the narrower direction and  the distance from the center to the co  vertex or the minor vertex we call that  distance B and then the distance from  the center to the foci we call that  distance C and it's all tied together  with this equation C squared equals a  squared minus B squared okay kind of  like Pythagorean theorem but you're  subtracting and then the general form of  the equation of ellipse is this guy  right here notice the h and the K picks  up the graph and it shifts it left and  right up and down but it has the  opposite effect so if it's X minus 2  you're actually shifting right - if it's  y plus 3 you're actually shifting down 3  ok that always equals 1 and then with  these denominators the a is always going  to be greater than B okay so just FYI  these can actually switch ok their  position and then the last thing is  talking about the eccentricities  eccentricities just gives you an idea  about how round the ellipses or how like  narrow and stretch you know and thin the  ellipses and the formula for  eccentricity is equal to C divided by a  so the distance to the from the center  to the focus divided by the distance  from the center to the vertex and an  easy and fun way to kind of remember  this is that the eccentricity for an  ellipse is in between 0 and 1 the closer  the eccentricities to 0 the more the  ellipse looks like a 0 so like a circle  okay like the like the number 0 and the  closer gets to 1 the you know more  stretched and thin it is it looks more  like the number 1 so you can kind of see  you know that's an easy way to kind of  remember you know what you're dealing  with when you're dealing with  eccentricity so before we get into this  example let me just mention that if  you're preparing for the AC T or the SAT  check out my huge a CT math review video  course and my huge SAT math review video  course I go through a number of  different concepts together with you we  go through example problem  there's a teaching section there's  opportunities for you to practice and if  you're prepared for that test and you  really want to boost your score a lot of  students have found a great benefit from  that so check out those courses but  let's go to this problem now and see if  we can solve it so they're giving us the  vertices are at plus or minus nine comma  zero so I always like to draw a sketch  just so I can see what we're dealing  with right  so plus or minus nine so we're going  right nine okay so I'll just write nine  zero so that's our vertex right there  and negative nine zero right so that's  our other vertex over here and the  eccentricity is two-thirds  now remember eccentricity is equal to C  divided by a so you can see we have a  little bit of an equation here that we  can work with the distance from the  center to the vertex this distance right  here remember is is our a and you can  see that a is equal to nine so if we put  nine in here we get two-thirds is equal  to C over nine right and if we multiply  both sides by nine you can see that C is  going to be equal to eighteen thirds  which is six okay so now we know what a  is it's nine we know it C is its six we  can solve for B by using this equation  right here C squared equals a squared  minus B squared so let's go ahead and do  that so we've gotten C squared equals a  squared minus B squared C squared would  be 6 squared which is 36 a squared is 9  squared which is 81 minus B squared and  let's see if we subtract 81 we get a  negative 45 equals a negative B squared  multiply both sides by negative one and  you can see that B squared is 45 if we  take the square root that's going to be  around less than seven okay so it's  gonna be somewhere around here and  around here so we can kind of get an  idea about the shape of our ellipse  something like this okay but we're  really after the equation in this  problem so let's take a look so it's  centered right at the origin so we don't  have to worry about the h and the K H  and K are going to be 0 this equation is  going to be x squared plus y squared  equals 1 and you can see we're going  nine to get to the vertices in the X  direction so that's going to be 9  squared is 81 and then in the Y  direction we're going to square root of  45 this is our B squared value and  that's going to be 45 so  that was a kind of a whirlwind tour on  how to solve for the equation of ellipse  when you're giving the eccentricities C  over a and the vertices so I hope that  helped you understand how to work with  these a little bit better if it was kind  of confusing definitely check out the  other videos that I did talking about  ellipses I'll have links for that for  you subscribe to the channel check out  more videos on my Mario's math tutoring  YouTube channel and I look forward to  helping you in the future videos I'll  talk to you soon
 

